eason":2020,"team":"CLE","distance":0.6738,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2021,"team":"PHI","distance":0.6887,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Jonathan Gannon"},{"season":2019,"team":"CAR","distance":0.7258,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2018,"team":"CIN","distance":0.7457,"shared":18,"play_caller":null}]},"overview":{"overview":"The 49ers went 6-11 in 2024 and were outscored 436 to 389. The offense carried the team, ranking 10th in EPA per play while the defense finished 23rd in EPA allowed. What set them apart was the highest 21 personnel usage and the lowest Cover 0 rate in the league.","offense":"The 49ers offense was well above average by EPA per play. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Schematically, they stood out for the highest 21 personnel usage and the 3rd highest pre-snap motion rate in the league. Brock Purdy ran the passing game, with Jauan Jennings leading the team in targets.","defense":"The 49ers defense was well below average by EPA allowed per play. It was much better against the pass than the run, ranking 17th and 29th. Nick Bosa was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was Leonard Floyd, who had 8.5 sacks while playing only 57% of the snaps, after not being a regular on this defense last season. Schematically, they had the lowest Cover 0 rate in the league.","special":"The 49ers were one of the most conservative teams on fourth down, going for it on only 19% of their short-yardage chances. Their kickers made 77% of field goals, which was well below average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into running backs, about 7% of the money on the books. Their offensive line was among the oldest in the league.","players":"Brock Purdy played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 3,864 yards. Jauan Jennings was the clear top target with 22% of the looks and 975 receiving yards. The backfield was a committee led by Jordan Mason and Isaac Guerendo. On defense, Nick Bosa led the team with 9 sacks and Fred Warner picked off 2 passes.","coaching":"Kyle Shanahan called the offense, and the defensive play caller for this season is not in the coaching data. Their offense looked most like the 2023 Texans under Bobby Slowik and the 2022 Raiders under Josh McDaniels. Their defense looked most like the 2018 Seahawks and the 2020 Browns.","trends":"The offense went from 1st in EPA per play in 2023 to 10th in 2024, a big step back. The defense moved from 7th in EPA allowed per play in 2023 to 23rd in 2024, a big step back. The biggest change in approach was fourth down go rate, which went from 40% to 19%.","notes":"Every data source is available for 2024. This season uses the newer formation labels, which group singleback, I formation and empty sets together as under center, so those older formation rates are not shown."}}